    "description": "Schema Forge analyzes versioned documents containing Paragraph, Heading, Image, Link, and Table nodes. Core releases ship accessibility checks, word-count metrics, Markdown export, and index extraction, while dialect packages independently add Callout, Citation, MathBlock, and custom note nodes. Existing operations must continue to process core nodes when an unknown dialect node appears, and a newly installed dialect must not force every core operation package to be recompiled or redeployed. Conversely, a new operation plugin must be installable without editing each concrete node kind. The current core traversal contract names every core concrete type, creating a dependency cycle between the element API and operation packages; it also treats every new operation as another full implementation of that contract. The requested design must preserve normal behavior for known node kinds, provide a deliberate fallback for unknown ones, and allow dialect-specific behavior to override the default only where intended. Integration tests will install a dialect and operation in either order and verify that all prebuilt core operations still load and run unchanged.",
    "decision_statement": "Whether to replace the closed two-way contract with an extension mechanism that lets new node kinds and new operations arrive independently while preserving existing packages.",
    "notes": "Extensible-visitor fits independently arriving node kinds and operations with fallback for unknown nodes; acyclic-visitor is the near-miss (element-side extension without recompiling visitors) and dispatch-table supports kind routing. Removed classic visitor from acceptable primaries because the description explicitly rejects the closed two-way contract it requires."
  },
